import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The auth package, a plugin: the package's example app file by file, how signing in goes (a mailed
## link, a provider, the dev sign-in), who gets in, sessions and the middleware, two-factor sign-in,
## the providers, what a failed sign-in says, the tables, safety, and what comes next. Static:
## signing in needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Auth",
	description: "Sign-in for Roc Solid apps, as a plugin: by a mailed link or at Google, GitHub and any OpenID Connect provider; two-factor sign-in with an authenticator app; sessions kept as hashed tokens in the app's database; middleware that hands the signed-in user on. No pages: the app's own.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How signing in goes" },
		{ id: "admit", label: "Who gets in" },
		{ id: "sessions", label: "Sessions and middleware" },
		{ id: "impersonation", label: "Impersonation" },
		{ id: "two-factor", label: "Two-factor sign-in" },
		{ id: "providers", label: "Providers" },
		{ id: "failed", label: "When it fails" },
		{ id: "tables", label: "The tables" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/auth</code>]}
				title="Sign-in you don't write yourself"
			>
				People sign in by a link mailed to their address, or at Google, GitHub or any OpenID Connect
				provider, and with a code from an authenticator app too once they turn that on. Sessions live in
				your app's database. Middleware hands the signed-in user to your routes. The package is a plugin,
				and has no pages: your sign-in page is yours.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Signing in is easy to write and easy to get wrong: tokens kept in plain text, links that work
				twice, a redirect that sends people off to another site, OAuth without its state or PKCE. The
				package does those parts. Your app keeps three: the pages, the email the link goes out in, and
				<code>admit!</code>, which says who gets in and which of your users they are.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				The package has a small app, <code>packages/auth/example</code>, and each file below is that app's.
				Its tests sign in every way, on SQLite and on Postgres, against a stand-in provider.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. The plugin.</strong> Name the package in the app's header and list its plugin with your
				<code>admit!</code>. The email plugin sends the links (with no SMTP server, under <code>--dev</code>,
				they land in the <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_email}#mailbox"}>dev mailbox</a>).
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_main.file} source={Sources.auth_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>2. Who gets in.</strong> <code>admit!</code> is given who is signing in, and answers with your
				id for their user, or why not. Here anyone gets in except addresses at <code>refused.example</code>, and
				a new address gets a new row in the app's <code>users</code> table:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_accounts.file} source={Sources.auth_example_accounts.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>3. The middleware.</strong> <code>Auth.required</code> for routes only signed-in people may
				see, <code>Auth.optional</code> for routes anyone may. Each takes a function that puts the session
				into the context your routes read:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_middlewares.file} source={Sources.auth_example_middlewares.rocx} />
			<p>
				A directory's <code>+middleware.roc</code> puts one around the routes in it. The example's
				signed-in routes sit in a group, <code>routes/(signed-in)/</code>, which adds nothing to their
				paths; the sign-in pages sit outside it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_signed_in.file} source={Sources.auth_example_signed_in.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>4. The sign-in page's endpoint</strong>, under <code>api/</code> at the page's path
				(<code>/api/sign-in</code>). <code>Auth.link!</code> keeps a link and gives you its URL, and you
				mail it as you like. Its <code>GET</code>, which the page reads, lists the providers that are set
				up, for their buttons:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_sign_in.file} source={Sources.auth_example_sign_in.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>5. The page a link opens.</strong> With <code>link_page</code>, a mailed link opens your page,
				which shows whom it signs in and posts the link to the plugin from a button. Some mail services
				open every link in a message to check it, and a link that signed in when opened would be used up
				before its person clicked it.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_link.file} source={Sources.auth_example_link.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_link_page.file} source={Sources.auth_example_link_page.rocx} />

			<Heading id="how">How signing in goes</Heading>
			<p>
				<strong>By a mailed link.</strong> <code>{"Auth.link!(email, then)"}</code> keeps the link in the
				database (its token's hash, the address, where it goes after, until when) and gives you the URL. A
				link works once, for 15 minutes (<code>link_ms</code>). Your page posts its token to
				<code>/auth/link</code>; without a <code>link_page</code>, the URL is <code>/auth/link</code> itself.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>At a provider.</strong> Your button links to <code>{"/auth/<provider>?then=…"}</code>. The
				plugin keeps a state, a PKCE verifier and a nonce in a cookie for ten minutes and sends the browser
				to the provider. Back at <code>{"/auth/<provider>/callback"}</code>, the state must be the one this
				browser kept. The code is traded for who signed in: an ID token (OpenID Connect), or GitHub's API.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Under <code>--dev</code>.</strong> <code>POST /auth/dev</code> with any <code>email</code> signs
				it in at once, with no mail. Without <code>--dev</code> there is no such endpoint.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Your own way.</strong> <code>{"Auth.sign_in!(answer, user)"}</code> starts a session for a user
				you decided on yourself (a password you checked, a person picked from a list) and sets its cookie.
				Without <code>admit!</code>, that is the only way in.
			</p>
			<p>
				The other ways end the same: <code>admit!</code> is asked, a session starts, and the browser goes on to
				where it was going with the session cookie. Where it goes is always a path of your site: a link
				or a provider can't send anyone off to another one.
			</p>

			<Heading id="admit">Who gets in</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>admit!</code> is given an <code>Auth.Identity</code>:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>provider : Str</code></td><td><code>"email"</code> for a link or the dev sign-in, else the provider's name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>subject : Str</code></td><td>who they are there: the address, or the provider's own id for them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>email : Str</code></td><td>their address, lower-cased; <code>""</code> when the provider has none it says is verified</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td>their name, or <code>""</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>user : [New, Known(Str)]</code></td><td>the user this identity signed in as before, even if its address has changed since</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>then : Str</code></td><td>where the sign-in goes after: an invite's page, say, which <code>admit!</code> may act on</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				It answers <code>Ok(user)</code>, your id for their user, or <code>Err(why)</code>, which the sign-in
				page shows. It runs inside the transaction that starts the session, so what it writes commits
				with the session (a new user's row, a welcome email queued as a job) or with its refusal (a
				request to join a waiting list). A database error rolls it all back. Keep slow work, such as a
				request to another server, out of it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="sessions">Sessions and middleware</Heading>
			<p>
				A session is a random token in a cookie, and a row in the database that keeps only its SHA-256.
				It lasts 30 days (<code>session_ms</code>). One used in its second half is kept for as long again,
				so a request doesn't write to the database each time.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>Auth.required</code> lets a signed-in request through. A signed-out page goes to your sign-in
					page and comes back after (<code>?then=</code>); anything else gets a 401 with the plugin's
					<code>refusal</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Auth.required_with</code> does the same, with a step that reads your user's row: a session
					whose user is gone ends, and answers as signed out. The example's <code>Middlewares.roc</code>
					above uses it.
				</li>
				<li><code>Auth.optional</code> hands on <code>SignedIn(session)</code> or <code>SignedOut</code>.</li>
				<li>
					<code>Auth.sessions!</code> lists a user's sessions, <code>Auth.revoke!</code> ends one, and
					<code>Auth.sign_out_everywhere!</code> ends them all.
				</li>
				<li>A form that posts to <code>/auth/sign-out</code> signs out.</li>
				<li>
					Your own way in: <code>{"Auth.sign_in!(answer, user)"}</code> after a password you checked,
					<code>{"Auth.switch!(request, answer, user)"}</code> when someone picks another person, and
					<code>{"Auth.path!(then)"}</code> for where they may go after.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="impersonation">Impersonation</Heading>
			<p>
				An admin helping someone can see the app as they see it.
				<code>{"Auth.impersonate!(request, user, ms)"}</code> makes the admin's session act as that user for a
				while: every route sees them. The session stays the admin's: <code>session.real_user</code> is the
				admin, it goes back when its time runs out or on <code>Auth.stop_impersonating!</code>, and each
				start and stop is on the log with both users. Who may impersonate is yours to decide before you
				call it; the example lets the addresses on <code>ADMIN_EMAILS</code>. Record who did what, and guard
				what only a person may change themselves, by <code>real_user</code>. An admin acting as someone
				with two-factor sign-in on isn't asked for their code: nobody signs in.
			</p>

			<Heading id="two-factor">Two-factor sign-in</Heading>
			<p>
				With <code>two_factor: Bool.True</code> in the plugin's options, your users may turn on two-factor
				sign-in. From then on, after their link or their provider, they enter the six-digit code their
				authenticator app shows (1Password, Google Authenticator, a phone's own passwords app), or one of
				their recovery codes. The codes are TOTP's (RFC 6238), which every authenticator app makes.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Turning it on.</strong> <code>{"Auth.two_factor_start!(user, account)"}</code> makes a secret and
				gives you its <code>otpauth://</code> URI, to show as a QR code (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_qr}>packages/qr</a>'s
				<code>QrCode</code>), and the secret as text, for an app that can't scan. The first code the app
				shows turns it on, and gives ten recovery codes, each good once in place of a code. Show them
				once: only their hashes are kept. The example's endpoints:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_two_factor.file} source={Sources.auth_example_two_factor.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_two_factor_confirm.file} source={Sources.auth_example_two_factor_confirm.rocx} />
			<p>
				<code>Auth.two_factor_disable!</code> turns it off and <code>Auth.two_factor_regenerate!</code> makes new
				recovery codes, each given a code the app shows now, or a recovery code.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Signing in.</strong> A user with it on isn't signed in by their link or provider yet: the
				sign-in waits ten minutes for their code, and the browser goes to your code page
				(<code>code_page</code>, <code>/sign-in/code</code>). Meanwhile your middleware treats them as signed
				out, but a page sends them to the code page, not the sign-in page. The code page posts the code to
				<code>/auth/code</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_code_page.file} source={Sources.auth_example_code_page.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					A code is the one the app shows now, or for the 30 seconds either side (a phone's clock a little
					out), and works once: a code already used is refused, even in time.
				</li>
				<li>A recovery code works once, and its use is on the log.</li>
				<li>
					A right code starts the session under a new token, so the one the browser waited with signs no
					one in. A wrong one comes back with <code>?error=code</code>; the fifth ends the sign-in.
				</li>
				<li>
					A user's wrong codes are counted across sign-ins too, and turning it off or making new recovery
					codes: after ten in a row their codes are refused for a quarter of an hour
					(<code>TooManyTries</code>, <code>?error=locked</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>Who must have it.</strong> The package doesn't decide: <code>{"Auth.two_factor!(user)"}</code> says
				whether it is on, and a route that wants it refuses those without it. The example's admins must
				have it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_admin.file} source={Sources.auth_example_admin.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>The secret at rest</strong> is sealed with the platform's <code>AesGcm</code>, under a key derived
				from your app's secret, <code>APP_SECRET</code> (32 characters or more: <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code>).
				With two-factor on, the server won't start without it, but under <code>--dev</code>, where a
				development secret stands in. Keep it: changed, every authenticator's codes stop working, and
				only recovery codes sign in.
			</p>
			<Note>
				The app <code>rocx new</code> makes has all of it: a section of its account page that sets it up with a
				QR code and turns it on and off, and its code page.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="providers">Providers</Heading>
			<p>
				Register an app with the provider, with the redirect URI below, and give the server its client id
				and secret. The URI starts with the server's public URL (<code>PUBLIC_URL</code>). A provider whose
				client id isn't set isn't offered: <code>Auth.providers!</code> leaves its button out.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Needs</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>Auth.google</code></td>
							<td><code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>; redirect URI <code>/auth/google/callback</code></td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>Auth.github</code></td>
							<td><code>GITHUB_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET</code>; callback URL <code>/auth/github/callback</code></td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>{"Auth.oidc({ name, label, issuer, authorize_url, token_url, client_id_env, client_secret_env })"}</code></td>
							<td>any OpenID Connect provider; redirect URI <code>{"/auth/<name>/callback"}</code></td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A record update changes any part of one: <code>{"{ ..Auth.google, client_id_env: \"ACME_GOOGLE_ID\" }"}</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="failed">When it fails</Heading>
			<p>
				A sign-in that fails goes to your sign-in page with <code>?error=</code>, and where it was going
				(<code>{"&then="}</code>), for the page to say what happened:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th><code>error</code></th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>link</code></td><td>the link has been used or has expired (an expired one still says where it was going)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>refused</code></td><td><code>admit!</code> said no; <code>{"&reason="}</code> is what it said, <code>{"&email="}</code> the address it turned away</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>provider</code></td><td>the provider refused, couldn't be reached, or isn't set up; <code>{"&reason="}</code> says which</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>state</code></td><td>back from a provider too late, or in another browser</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>unavailable</code></td><td>the database couldn't take it just then</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>attempts</code></td><td>the fifth wrong code ended a sign-in waiting for its code</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>locked</code></td><td>the user had given ten wrong codes in a row: their codes are refused for a quarter of an hour after the last</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td>no sign-in was waiting for a code in this browser: ten minutes went by</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>A wrong code goes back to your code page with <code>?error=code</code>.</p>
			<p>
				<code>auth.SignInError</code> has these sentences: <code>SignInError.message(query)</code> gives the one
				for a sign-in page's query, and <code>SignInError.field(query, "then")</code> reads a field. It names no
				platform, so your page uses it in the browser too.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tables">The tables</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin's migrations make its tables, the same on SQLite and on Postgres:
				<code>auth_sessions</code>, <code>auth_links</code> (links on their way),
				<code>auth_identities</code> (which of your users each identity signed in as), and for two-factor
				sign-in <code>auth_two_factor</code> (each sealed secret), <code>auth_recovery_codes</code> (their
				hashes) and <code>auth_waiting</code> (sign-ins waiting for a code). Your users stay in your own
				table; the package knows them only by the id <code>admit!</code> gives.
			</p>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>Tokens are 32 random bytes, kept only as their SHA-256: a leaked table signs no one in.</li>
				<li>A link is used and deleted in one statement, so two clicks can't both sign in.</li>
				<li>
					Cookies are <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, and on HTTPS <code>Secure</code>
					and <code>__Host-</code>, so no other site under your domain can set them.
				</li>
				<li>
					OAuth's state is compared in constant time, PKCE binds the code to the browser that asked, and the
					nonce binds the ID token to it. The ID token's issuer, audience and expiry are checked, and an
					address counts only if the provider says it is verified.
				</li>
				<li>Links in mail use the server's public URL, never a request's <code>Host</code>.</li>
				<li>
					An authenticator's secret is sealed (AES-256-GCM) and never kept in clear; recovery codes are 80
					random bits, kept as their SHA-256.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				A form on another site can't post to yours: the platform refuses it
				(<a href={"${Paths.safety}#origin"}>why</a>). Put a <a href={Paths.ecosystem_guard}>rate limit</a>
				on your sign-in endpoint, per address and per client (its directory's <code>+middleware.roc</code>),
				so nobody can mail an address a hundred links. The app <code>rocx new</code> makes has it.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>
				Links, the link page, Google, GitHub, OpenID Connect, the dev sign-in, sessions, the middleware,
				impersonation and two-factor sign-in are built and tested. Passwords come next: they need a slow
				hash (Argon2) that the server's host will compute. Passkeys come later: they need the host to
				verify a P-256 signature, which it can make but not yet check. The package's
				<code>PROPOSALS.md</code> has both.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
